WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.220 --> 00:00:03.570
Checkout succeeded. The order is in Postgres. But

2
00:00:03.570 --> 00:00:07.339
Kafka never received its event. How can that happen?

3
00:00:08.259 --> 00:00:11.385
Saving an order and publishing to Kafka are

4
00:00:11.385 --> 00:00:15.292
separate operations. A crash between them can lose the notification.

5
00:00:16.212 --> 00:00:19.452
Use the transactional outbox. Insert the order and

6
00:00:19.452 --> 00:00:22.693
its event in one database transaction. Both commit.

7
00:00:22.693 --> 00:00:24.313
or both roll back.

8
00:00:25.233 --> 00:00:28.146
Debezium reads the committed outbox insert from the

9
00:00:28.146 --> 00:00:31.059
database change log and publishes it to Kafka.

10
00:00:31.059 --> 00:00:32.516
keyed by order ID.

11
00:00:33.436 --> 00:00:37.712
Three independent consumer groups react: order management. customer

12
00:00:37.712 --> 00:00:42.523
notifications. and business analytics. A broker outage delays delivery.

13
00:00:43.443 --> 00:00:46.812
Retries may deliver the event again. Consumers deduplicate

14
00:00:46.812 --> 00:00:50.182
using the event ID. and commit business updates

15
00:00:50.182 --> 00:00:51.867
with the deduplication record.

16
00:00:52.787 --> 00:00:56.869
External email is another boundary. Use provider idempotency

17
00:00:56.869 --> 00:01:00.952
when available. plus retries and reconciliation. Never promise

18
00:01:00.952 --> 00:01:02.483
universal exactly once.

19
00:01:03.403 --> 00:01:07.158
Monitor connector lag. protect customer data. test outages.

20
00:01:07.158 --> 00:01:10.912
and alert on failures. Reliable commerce is designed

21
00:01:10.912 --> 00:01:13.259
for crashes. not perfect networks.

